# Spring Boot Zero Day Remediation Playbook

## 1. Purpose

Remediate all Spring Boot and Spring Framework components flagged in a Zero Day
event by upgrading to the approved version, validating via Black Duck scan, and
completing the OSS validation process. Scope is strictly limited to Spring
dependency version changes. No other code, logic, configuration, or dependency
is touched unless it directly blocks the Spring upgrade and the human explicitly
approves it.

> **Principle**: Make only the version changes that Black Duck and the approved
> Spring Framework sheet require. Never change application behavior, public
> contracts, security configuration, logging, telemetry, business logic, or test
> expectations unless the change is **required** for Spring remediation **and**
> the human explicitly approves it.

-----

## 2. Scope

This playbook applies to Java/Kotlin applications that contain one or more of:

- `spring-boot-starter-*` dependencies in any version
- `spring-framework` BOM or direct `org.springframework.*` dependencies
- `spring-security-*` dependencies
- `spring-cloud-*` dependencies
- `spring-data-*` dependencies
- Orchestra Spring Boot wrapper libraries (`orchestra-spring-boot-*`)

**In scope:**

- `pom.xml`, `build.gradle`, `build.gradle.kts`, `gradle.properties`,
  `gradle/libs.versions.toml` — version changes only
- Spring BOM import version in dependency management blocks
- Orchestra wrapper version (`orchestra-spring-boot-starter`)
- Parent POM version when `spring-boot-starter-parent` is the parent

**Out of scope — never touch without explicit human approval:**

- Application source code (`.java`, `.kt`)
- Test source code
- `application.yml` / `application.properties` (unless a property key was
  removed in the target Spring version and migration is required — stop and ask)
- Non-Spring dependencies
- Dockerfile, CI/CD pipeline, Kubernetes manifests
- Database migration scripts
- Architecture changes of any kind

> If a required file, import path, current version, or approved target version
> is unclear — stop and ask a precise question before making any change.

-----

## 3. Required Repository Discovery

Before editing any file, inspect and identify:

- All build files: `pom.xml` (root and modules), `build.gradle`,
  `build.gradle.kts`, `gradle.properties`, `gradle/libs.versions.toml`,
  `settings.gradle`, `settings.gradle.kts`
- The build system in use: Maven, Gradle, or both
- Whether the project is single-module or multi-module
- Current Spring Boot version (from `<parent>`, `spring-boot-starter-parent`,
  BOM import, or `springBootVersion` property)
- Current Spring Framework version if overridden separately
- Current Orchestra Spring Boot wrapper version if present
- All `spring-*` and `org.springframework.*` dependencies and their versions,
  including transitives that are explicitly overridden
- Whether versions are declared inline, in a `<properties>` block, in a version
  catalog, or inherited from a BOM
- Black Duck scan report if provided — extract the component name, current
  version, and flagged CVEs for each Spring component

-----

## 4. Version Resolution Rules

Apply exactly one upgrade path based on the current Spring Boot major version.
Do not mix paths. Do not skip a major version without human approval.

### 4.1 Spring Boot 4.x / Spring Framework 7.x

- Use the **single approved version** listed on the internal Spring Framework
  sheet for this event
- Do not pick a different minor or patch version
- Orchestra wrapper: upgrade to `4.1.5` unless the approved sheet specifies
  otherwise
- Java requirement: Java 17 or higher (do not change Java version — if current
  Java is below 17, stop and report a blocker)
- Tomcat requirement: Tomcat 11.x (do not change Tomcat version — if current
  Tomcat is below 11, stop and report a blocker)

### 4.2 Spring Boot 3.x / Spring Framework 6.x

- Multiple approved versions may be listed on the internal Spring Framework
  sheet
- Upgrade to the **latest available approved minor version** for the 3.x line
- Do not upgrade from 3.x to 4.x in this playbook — that requires a separate
  human-approved migration
- Orchestra wrapper: upgrade to `3.23.8` unless the approved sheet specifies
  otherwise
- Java requirement: Java 17 or higher (do not change Java version — if current
  Java is below 17, stop and report a blocker)
- Tomcat requirement: Tomcat 10.x (do not change Tomcat version — if current
  Tomcat is below 10, stop and report a blocker)

### 4.3 Spring Boot 2.x / Spring Framework 5.x

- **No major version upgrade is required within this 72-hour event window**
- Apply only the approved patch version for the 2.x line if one is listed
- If no 2.x patch is listed on the approved sheet, make no version change
- Post-event action required: a plan to migrate to Spring Boot 3.x or 4.x must
  be developed — document this in the remediation report but do not execute it
- Spring Boot 2.x is approaching end-of-life risk thresholds — record this as
  a risk item in the report

### 4.4 Java and Tomcat Upgrades

- For this event, Java and Tomcat upgrades are **not required** unless the
  current versions are significantly outdated and directly block the Spring
  Boot upgrade
- If Java or Tomcat must be upgraded to unblock Spring, stop and ask the human
  for approval before proceeding

-----

## 5. Build File Modification Rules

### 5.1 Maven (`pom.xml`)

**Case A — `spring-boot-starter-parent` as parent:**

```xml
<!-- Change only the version value. Touch nothing else. -->
<parent>
  <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
  <version>APPROVED_VERSION</version>
  <relativePath/>
</parent>
```

**Case B — Spring Boot BOM imported in `<dependencyManagement>`:**

```xml
<dependencyManagement>
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
      <artifactId>spring-boot-dependencies</artifactId>
      <version>APPROVED_VERSION</version>
      <type>pom</type>
      <scope>import</scope>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</dependencyManagement>
```

**Case C — Version declared in `<properties>`:**

```xml
<properties>
  <spring-boot.version>APPROVED_VERSION</spring-boot.version>
  <!-- or -->
  <spring.boot.version>APPROVED_VERSION</spring.boot.version>
</properties>
```

**Case D — Orchestra wrapper present:**

```xml
<dependency>
  <groupId>com.wellsfargo.orchestra</groupId>
  <artifactId>orchestra-spring-boot-starter</artifactId>
  <version>APPROVED_ORCHESTRA_VERSION</version>
</dependency>
```

**Rules:**

- Change only the version string. Do not reformat, reorder, or add/remove any
  other XML elements.
- If Spring Framework version is overridden separately via
  `<spring-framework.version>` property, update it to match the Spring
  Framework version paired with the approved Spring Boot version.
- If multiple `pom.xml` files exist (multi-module), update the root POM version
  declaration. Do not update child POMs unless they override the Spring version
  independently — if they do, update each override individually.
- Do not add a BOM if one does not exist. Do not restructure dependency
  management.

### 5.2 Gradle (`build.gradle` / `build.gradle.kts`)

**Case A — Plugin version:**

```groovy
// build.gradle
plugins {
  id 'org.springframework.boot' version 'APPROVED_VERSION'
}

// build.gradle.kts
plugins {
  id("org.springframework.boot") version "APPROVED_VERSION"
}
```

**Case B — Version in `gradle.properties`:**

```properties
springBootVersion=APPROVED_VERSION
```

**Case C — Version catalog (`gradle/libs.versions.toml`):**

```toml
[versions]
spring-boot = "APPROVED_VERSION"
```

**Rules:**

- Change only the version string. Do not reformat or restructure the build file.
- If `ext` block or `buildscript` block declares the Spring Boot version,
  update that property.
- For multi-project Gradle builds, update only the root version declaration
  unless subprojects override it independently.

