## 10. Stop Conditions

Stop immediately and ask the human a precise question if any of the following
are true:

- The approved target version is not provided or cannot be determined
- The current Spring Boot major version cannot be identified from build files
- A Java version below 17 is present and the target is Spring Boot 3.x or 4.x
- A Tomcat version below 10 is present and the target is Spring Boot 3.x,
  or below 11 for Spring Boot 4.x
- A child module declares a Spring version override that conflicts with the root
- A flagged Black Duck component is not a Spring component and requires a
  separate upgrade decision
- Any `application.yml` property key must be changed due to a removed or
  renamed Spring property in the target version
- The build file structure is non-standard and the authoritative version
  declaration is ambiguous
- More than one Spring Boot major version is present across modules

Do not guess. Do not assume an approved version. Do not proceed past a blocker.

-----

## 11. Principles Summary

- Only change what the Zero Day event requires
- Only change Spring dependency versions and Orchestra wrapper versions
- Never change application code, tests, or unrelated dependencies
- Never assume an approved version — it must be provided or confirmed
- Never upgrade across a major version boundary without explicit human approval
- Always produce a remediation report
- This will not be a one-time event — build changes must be clean, reviewable,
  and repeatable
